        <register>
        <name>MAC_PTO_CONTROL</name>
          <description>This register controls the PTP Offload Engine operation. This register is available only when the Enable PTP Timestamp Offload feature is selected.</description>
          <addressOffset>0xbc0</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
           <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DN</name>
              <description>This field indicates the domain Number in which the PTP node is operating.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PDRDIS</name>
              <description>When this bit is set, the Peer Delay Response (Pdelay_Resp) response is not be generated for received Peer Delay Request (Pdelay_Req) request packet, as required by the programmed mode.
Note: Setting this bit to 1 affects the normal PTP Offload operation and the time synchronization. So, this bit must be set only if there is problem with Pdelay_Resp generation in Hardware and/or Pdelay_Resp generation is handled by Software.</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Peer Delay Response response generation is enabled</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Peer Delay Response response generation is disabled</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DRRDIS</name>
              <description>When this bit is set, the Delay Request and Delay response is not generated for received SYNC and Delay request packet respectively, as required by the programmed mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>PTO Delay Request/Response response generation is enabled</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>PTO Delay Request/Response response generation is disabled</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>APDREQTRIG</name>
              <description>When this bit is set, one PTP Pdelay_Req message is transmitted. This bit is automatically cleared after the PTP Pdelay_Req message is transmitted. The application should set the APDREQEN bit for this operation.
Access restriction applies. Setting 1 sets. Self-cleared. Setting 0 has no effect.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Automatic PTP Pdelay_Req message Trigger is disabled</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Automatic PTP Pdelay_Req message Trigger is enabled</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ASYNCTRIG</name>
              <description>When this bit is set, one PTP SYNC message is transmitted. This bit is automatically cleared after the PTP SYNC message is transmitted. The application should set the ASYNCEN bit for this operation.
Access restriction applies. Setting 1 sets. Self-cleared. Setting 0 has no effect.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Automatic PTP SYNC message Trigger is disabled</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Automatic PTP SYNC message Trigger is enabled</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>APDREQEN</name>
              <description>When this bit is set, PTP Pdelay_Req message is generated periodically based on interval programmed or trigger from application, when the MAC is programmed to be in Peer-to-Peer Transparent mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Automatic PTP Pdelay_Req message is disabled</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Automatic PTP Pdelay_Req message is enabled</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ASYNCEN</name>
              <description>When this bit is set, PTP SYNC message is generated periodically based on interval programmed or trigger from application, when the MAC is programmed to be in Clock Master mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Automatic PTP SYNC message is disabled</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Automatic PTP SYNC message is enabled</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PTOEN</name>
              <description>When this bit is set, the PTP Offload feature is enabled.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>PTP Offload feature is disabled</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>PTP Offload feature is enabled</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>

        <register>
        <name>MAC_SOURCE_PORT_IDENTITY</name>
          <description>This register contains Bits[31:0] of the 80-bit Source Port Identity of the PTP node. This register is available only when the Enable PTP Timestamp Offload feature is selected.</description>
          <addressOffset>0xbc4</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
           <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SPI</name>
              <description>This field indicates bits [31:0] of sourcePortIdentity of PTP node.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>32</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>

        <register>
        <name>MAC_LOG_MESSAGE_INTERVAL</name>
          <description>This register contains the periodic intervals for automatic PTP packet generation. This register is available only when the Enable PTP Timestamp Offload feature is selected.</description>
          <addressOffset>0xbd0</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
           <fields>
            <field>
              <name>LMPDRI</name>
              <description>This field indicates logMinPdelayReqInterval of PTP node.
This is used to schedule the periodic Pdelay request packet transmission. Allowed values are -15 to 15.Negative value must be represented in 2's-complement form. For example, if the required value is -1, the value programmed must be 0xFF.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DRSYNCR</name>
              <description>In Slave mode, it is used for controlling frequency of Delay_Req messages transmitted.
■ 0: DelayReq generated for every received SYNC
■ 1: DelayReq generated every alternate reception of SYNC
■ 2: for every 4 SYNC messages
■ 3: for every 8 SYNC messages
■ 4: for every 16 SYNC messages
■ 5: for every 32 SYNC messages
■ 6-7: Reserved
The master sends this information (logMinDelayReqInterval) in the DelayResp PTP messages to the slave. The DWC_ether_qos Receiver processes this value from the received DelayResp messages and updates this field accordingly. In the Slave mode, the host must not write/update this register unless it has to override the received value. In Master mode, the sum of this field and logSyncInterval (LSI) field is provided in the logMinDelayReqInterval field of the generated multicast Delay_Resp PTP message.
Access restriction applies. Updated based on the event. Setting 1 sets. Setting 0 clears.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>3</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>DelayReq generated for every received SYNC</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>DelayReq generated every alternate reception of SYNC</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>2</name>
                  <description>for every 4 SYNC messages</description>
                  <value>2</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>3</name>
                  <description>for every 8 SYNC messages</description>
                  <value>3</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>4</name>
                  <description>for every 16 SYNC messages</description>
                  <value>4</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>5</name>
                  <description>for every 32 SYNC messages</description>
                  <value>5</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>6</name>
                  <description>Reserved</description>
                  <value>6</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LSI</name>
              <description>This field indicates the periodicity of the automatically generated SYNC message when the PTP node is Master. Allowed values are -15 to 15. Negative value must be represented in 2's-complement form. For example, if the required value is -1, the value programmed must be 0xFF.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>

        <register>
        <name>MTL_OPERATION_MODE</name>
          <description></description>
          <addressOffset>0xc00</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
           <fields>
            <field>
              <name>FRPE</name>
              <description>When this bit is set to 1, the Programmable Rx Parser functionality is enabled.
When the Rx parser is disabled and if the Rx parser is in the middle of the parsing then it gets disabled only after completing the current packet parsing.
When the Rx parser is enabled from disabled state then the Rx parser gets activated for the next immediate packet.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Flexible Rx parser is disabled</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Flexible Rx parser is enabled</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CNTCLR</name>
              <description>When this bit is set, all counters are reset. This bit is cleared automatically after 1 clock cycle.
If this bit is set along with CNT_PRESET bit, CNT_PRESET has precedence.
Access restriction applies. Setting 1 sets. Self-cleared. Setting 0 has no effect.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Counters are not reset</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Counters are reset</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CNTPRST</name>
              <description>When this bit is set,
■ MTL_TxQ[0-7]_Underflow register is initialized/preset to 12'h7F0.
■ Missed Packet and Overflow Packet counters in MTL_RxQ[0-7]_Missed_Packet_Overflow_Cnt register is initialized/preset to 12'h7F0.
Access restriction applies. Setting 1 sets. Self-cleared. Setting 0 has no effect.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Counters Preset is disabled</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Counters Preset is enabled</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SCHALG</name>
              <description>This field indicates the algorithm for Tx scheduling:</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>WRR algorithm</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>WFQ algorithm when DCB feature is selected.Otherwise, Reserved</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>2</name>
                  <description>DWRR algorithm when DCB feature is selected.Otherwise, Reserved</description>
                  <value>2</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>3</name>
                  <description>Strict priority algorithm</description>
                  <value>3</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RAA</name>
              <description>This field is used to select the arbitration algorithm for the Rx side.
■ 0: Strict priority (SP)
Queue 0 has the lowest priority and the last queue has the highest priority.
■ 1: Weighted Strict Priority (WSP)</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Strict priority (SP)</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Weighted Strict Priority (WSP)</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DTXSTS</name>
              <description>When this bit is set, the Tx packet status received from the MAC is dropped in the MTL. When this bit is reset, the Tx packet status received from the MAC is forwarded to the application.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Drop Transmit Status is disabled</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Drop Transmit Status is enabled</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>

        <register>
        <name>MTL_DBG_CTL</name>
          <description>The FIFO Debug Access Control and Status register controls the operation mode of FIFO debug access.</description>
          <addressOffset>0xc08</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
           <fields>
            <field>
              <name>EIEC</name>
              <description>When EIEE bit of this register is set, following are the errors inserted based on the value encoded in this field.</description>
              <bitOffset>17</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Insert 1 bit error</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Insert 2 bit error</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>2</name>
                  <description>Insert 3 bit error</description>
                  <value>2</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>3</name>
                  <description>Insert 1 bit error in address field</description>
                  <value>3</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EIEE</name>
              <description>When set, enables the ECC error injection feature. When reset, disables the ECC error injection feature.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>ECC Inject Error for Tx, Rx and TSO memories is disabled</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>ECC Inject Error for Tx, Rx and TSO memories is enabled</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>STSIE</name>
              <description>When this bit is set, an interrupt is generated when Transmit status is available in slave mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Transmit Packet Available Interrupt Status is disabled</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Transmit Packet Available Interrupt Status is enabled</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PKTIE</name>
              <description>When this bit is set, an interrupt is generated when EOP of received packet is written to the Rx FIFO.</description>
              <bitOffset>14</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Receive Packet Available Interrupt Status is disabled</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Receive Packet Available Interrupt Status is enabled</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>FIFOSEL</name>
              <description>This field indicates the FIFO selected for debug access:</description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Tx FIFO</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Tx Status FIFO (only read access when SLVMOD is set)</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>2</name>
                  <description>TSO FIFO (cannot be accessed when SLVMOD is set)</description>
                  <value>2</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>3</name>
                  <description>Rx FIFO</description>
                  <value>3</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>FIFOWREN</name>
              <description>When this bit is set, it enables the Write operation on selected FIFO when FIFO Debug Access is enabled.
This bit must not be written to 1 when FIFO Debug Access is not enabled, that is FDBGEN bit is 0.
Access restriction applies. Self-cleared. Setting 0 clears. Setting 1 sets.</description>
              <bitOffset>11</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>FIFO Write is disabled</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>FIFO Write is enabled</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>FIFORDEN</name>
              <description>When this bit is set, it enables the Read operation on selected FIFO when FIFO Debug Access is enabled.
This bit must not be written to 1 when FIFO Debug Access is not enabled, that is FDBGEN bit is 0.
Access restriction applies. Self-cleared. Setting 0 clears. Setting 1 sets.</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>FIFO Read is disabled</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>FIFO Read is enabled</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RSTSEL</name>
              <description>When this bit is set, the pointers of the currently-selected FIFO are reset when FIFO Debug Access is enabled.
This bit must not be written to 1 when FIFO Debug Access is not enabled, that is FDBGEN bit is 0.
Access restriction applies. Self-cleared. Setting 0 clears. Setting 1 sets.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Reset Pointers of Selected FIFO is disabled</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Reset Pointers of Selected FIFO is enabled</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RSTALL</name>
              <description>When this bit is set, the pointers of all FIFOs are reset when FIFO Debug Access is enabled.
This bit must not be written to 1 when FIFO Debug Access is not enabled, that is FDBGEN bit is 0.
Access restriction applies. Self-cleared. Setting 0 clears. Setting 1 sets.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Reset All Pointers is disabled</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Reset All Pointers is enabled</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PKTSTATE</name>
              <description>This field is used to write the control information to the Tx FIFO or Rx FIFO.
Tx FIFO:
■ 00: Packet Data
■ 01: Control Word
■ 10: SOP Data
■ 11: EOP Data
Rx FIFO:
■ 00: Packet Data
■ 01: Normal Status
■ 10: Last Status
■ 11: EOP</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Packet Data</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Control Word/Normal Status</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>2</name>
                  <description>SOP Data/Last Status</description>
                  <value>2</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>3</name>
                  <description>EOP Data/EOP</description>
                  <value>3</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BYTEEN</name>
              <description>This field indicates the number of data bytes valid in the data register during Write operation. This is valid only when PKTSTATE is 2'b10 (EOP) and Tx FIFO or Rx FIFO is selected.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Byte 0 valid</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Byte 0 and Byte 1 are valid</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>2</name>
                  <description>Byte 0, Byte 1 and Byte 2 are valid</description>
                  <value>2</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>3</name>
                  <description>All four bytes are valid</description>
                  <value>3</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DBGMOD</name>
              <description>When this bit is set, it indicates that the current access to the FIFO is read, write, and debug access. In this mode, the following access types are allowed:
■ Read and Write access to Tx FIFO, TSO FIFO, and Rx FIFO
■ Read access is allowed to Tx Status FIFO.
When this bit is reset, it indicates that the current access to the FIFO is slave access bypassing the DMA. In this mode, the following access are allowed:
■ Write access to the Tx FIFO
■ Read access to the Rx FIFO and Tx Status FIFO</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Debug Mode Access to FIFO is disabled</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Debug Mode Access to FIFO is enabled</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>FDBGEN</name>
              <description>When this bit is set, it indicates that the debug mode access to the FIFO is enabled. When this bit is reset, it indicates that the FIFO can be accessed only through a master interface.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>FIFO Debug Access is disabled</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>FIFO Debug Access is enabled</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>

        <register>
        <name>MTL_DBG_STS</name>
          <description>The FIFO Debug Status register contains the status of FIFO debug access.</description>
          <addressOffset>0xc0c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000018</resetValue>
           <fields>
            <field>
              <name>LOCR</name>
              <description>Slave Access Mode:
This field indicates the space available in selected FIFO. Debug Access Mode:
This field contains the Write or Read pointer value of the selected FIFO during Write or Read operation, respectively. Reset: In single Tx Queue configurations, (DWC_EQOS_TXFIFO_SIZE/(DWC_EQOS_DATAWIDTH/8)), Otherwise 0000H</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>17</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>STSI</name>
              <description>When set, this bit indicates that the Slave mode Tx packet is transmitted, and the status is available in Tx Status FIFO. This bit is reset when 1 is written to this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Transmit Status Available Interrupt Status not detected</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Transmit Status Available Interrupt Status detected</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PKTI</name>
              <description>When set, this bit indicates that MAC layer has written the EOP of received packet to the Rx FIFO. This bit is reset when 1 is written to this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Receive Packet Available Interrupt Status not detected</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Receive Packet Available Interrupt Status detected</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BYTEEN</name>
              <description>This field indicates the number of data bytes valid in the data register during Read operation. This is valid only when PKTSTATE is 2'b10 (EOP) and Tx FIFO or Rx FIFO is selected.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Byte 0 valid</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Byte 0 and Byte 1 are valid</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>2</name>
                  <description>Byte 0, Byte 1 and Byte 2 are valid</description>
                  <value>2</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>3</name>
                  <description>All four bytes are valid</description>
                  <value>3</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PKTSTATE</name>
              <description>This field is used to get the control or status information of the selected FIFO.
Tx FIFO:
■ 00: Packet Data
■ 01: Control Word
■ 10: SOP Data
■ 11: EOP Data
Rx FIFO:
■ 00: Packet Data
■ 01: Normal Status
■ 10: Last Status
■ 11: EOP
This field is applicable only for Tx FIFO and Rx FIFO during Read operation.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Packet Data</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Control Word/Normal Status</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>2</name>
                  <description>SOP Data/Last Status</description>
                  <value>2</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>3</name>
                  <description>EOP Data/EOP</description>
                  <value>3</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>FIFOBUSY</name>
              <description>When set, this bit indicates that a FIFO operation is in progress in the MAC and content of the following fields is not valid:
■ All other fields of this register
■ All fields of the MTL_FIFO_Debug_Data register</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>FIFO Busy not detected</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>FIFO Busy detected</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>

        <register>
        <name>MTL_DBG_DATA</name>
          <description>The FIFO Debug Data register contains the data to be written to or read from the FIFOs.</description>
          <addressOffset>0xc10</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
           <fields>
            <field>
              <name>FDBGDATA</name>
              <description>During debug or slave access write operation, this field contains the data to be written to the Tx FIFO, Rx FIFO, or TSO FIFO. During debug or slave access read operation, this field contains the data read from the Tx FIFO, Rx FIFO, TSO FIFO, or Tx Status FIFO.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>32</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>

        <register>
        <name>MTL_INTERRUPT_STATUS</name>
          <description>The software driver (application) reads this register during interrupt service routine or polling to determine the interrupt status of MTL queues and the MAC.</description>
          <addressOffset>0xc20</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
           <fields>
            <field>
              <name>MTLPIS</name>
              <description>This bit indicates that there is an interrupt from Rx Parser Block. To reset this bit, the application must read the MTL_ Rxp_Interrupt_Status register to get the exact cause of the interrupt and clear its source.</description>
              <bitOffset>23</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>MTL Rx Parser Interrupt status not detected</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>MTL Rx Parser Interrupt status detected</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ESTIS</name>
              <description>This bit indicates an interrupt event during the operation of 802.1Qbv. To reset this bit, the application must clear the error/event that has caused the Interrupt.</description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>EST (TAS- 802.1Qbv) Interrupt status not detected</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>EST (TAS- 802.1Qbv) Interrupt status detected</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DBGIS</name>
              <description>This bit indicates an interrupt event during the slave access. To reset this bit, the application must read the FIFO Debug Access Status register to get the exact cause of the interrupt and clear its source.</description>
              <bitOffset>17</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Debug Interrupt status not detected</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Debug Interrupt status detected</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MACIS</name>
              <description>This bit indicates an interrupt event from the MAC. To reset this bit, the application must read the MAC Interrupt Status register to get the exact cause of the interrupt and clear its source.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>MAC Interrupt status not detected</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>MAC Interrupt status detected</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>QnIS</name>
              <description>This bit indicates that there is an interrupt from Queue n. To reset this bit, the application must read the MTL_Qn_Interrupt_Control_Status Status register to get the exact cause of the interrupt and clear its source. One bit for one queue.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>

        <register>
        <name>MTL_RXQ_DMA_MAP0</name>
          <description>The Receive Queue and DMA Channel Mapping 0 register is reserved in EQOS-CORE and EQOS-MTL configurations.</description>
          <addressOffset>0xc30</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
           <fields>
            <field>
              <name>Q3DDMACH</name>
              <description>When set, this bit indicates that the packets received in Queue 3 are routed to a particular DMA channel as decided in the MAC Receiver based on the DMA channel number programmed in the L3-L4 filter registers, or the Ethernet DA address.
When reset, this bit indicates that the packets received in Queue 3 are routed to the DMA Channel programmed in the Q3MDMACH field (Bits[26:24]).</description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Queue 3 disabled for DA-based DMA Channel Selection</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Queue 3 enabled for DA-based DMA Channel Selection</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>Q3MDMACH</name>
              <description>This field controls the routing of the received packet in Queue 3 to the DMA Channel.
This field is valid when the Q3DDMACH field is reset.
Note: The width of this field depends on the number of RX DMA channels and not all the values may be valid in some configurations. For example, if the number of RX DMA channels selected is 2, only 000 and 001 are valid, the others are reserved</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>3</bitWidth>
